{
  "_comment": "Emoji-only messages shown large (docs/EMOJI.md §7, M101). The rule every client applies (desktop ui/emojiOnly.ts, iOS UI/EmojiOnly.swift, Android ui/EmojiOnly.kt; each keeps a copy of `max_items`, `whitespace` and `pictographic`, and its test compares them with this file and runs `cases`). 1) Standard shortcodes are replaced first (`:tada:` -> the glyph, apps/shared/emoji.json, as the body is rendered). 2) Walk the text by code point: whitespace (`whitespace`) separates items; `:name:` (name ^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9_+-]{1,31}$) that is a known custom emoji is one item (kind `text` for a text emoji, `pack` for an image emoji with a pack_id, else `image`); a keycap ([0-9#*] FE0F? 20E3), a flag (two regional indicators 1F1E6-1F1FF) or an emoji sequence is one `unicode` item. An emoji sequence is element (200D element)*; an element is a `pictographic` code point, then FE0F?, then a skin tone (1F3FB-1F3FF)?, and after 1F3F4 tag characters (E0020-E007E)* E007F. Anything else (text, markdown, a mention, an unknown :x:, a lone modifier or joiner) -> not emoji-only. 3) Emoji-only = 1 to `max_items` items. `stamp` = exactly one item and it is `pack`. `pictographic` = the Unicode Emoji property (Unicode 17) without ASCII, regional indicators, skin tones and the joiner / VS16 / keycap / tag characters, plus all of 1F000-1FAFF (emoji not yet assigned there count too). `custom` is the custom emoji table the cases are checked with; `why` is only for people.",
